---
import PageLayout from "@/layouts/Base.astro";

const meta = {
	description: "About Arthur Jordão - software engineer writing Haskell and Clojure in production.",
	title: "About",
};
---

<PageLayout meta={meta}>
	<h1 class="title mb-12">About</h1>
	<div class="prose prose-sm prose-cactus max-w-none">
		<p>
			Hello, my name is Arthur. I&rsquo;m a software engineer at <a
				class="cactus-link"
				href="https://www.noredink.com/"
				rel="noreferrer"
				target="_blank">NoRedInk</a
			>, where I&rsquo;ve written Haskell in production for the last four years. Before that,
			Clojure at <a
				class="cactus-link"
				href="https://www.nubank.com.br"
				rel="noreferrer"
				target="_blank">Nubank</a
			>. Most of my career has been functional programming at companies that run it at scale &mdash;
			earlier on, <a
				class="cactus-link"
				href="https://www.catho.com.br"
				rel="noreferrer"
				target="_blank">Catho</a
			> and <a
				class="cactus-link"
				href="https://accenture.com"
				rel="noreferrer"
				target="_blank">Accenture</a
			>.
		</p>
		<p>
			I&rsquo;ve been coding since I was 12. My main languages are <code>Clojure</code>, <code
				>Haskell</code
			> and <code>Elm</code>.
		</p>
		<p>
			Outside work I run a small homelab, managed the way I like code to be: templated, validated
			and reproducible rather than hand-tuned.
		</p>
		<p>
			I love the idea that you can build anything with software, good abstractions, and <del
				>a lot of</del
			> some patience. The beginning is hard, and there are a lot of tries and fail in the middle,
			but the feeling of getting different concepts and combining them together until you build
			something is like playing Lego, and that is the feeling that makes me love programming.
		</p>
		<p>
			In my free time I mostly go to the gym, cook some delicious cakes, meditate, watch some
			series, do some yoga, play chess, and so on.
		</p>
	</div>
</PageLayout>
